The number of electrons that can be placed in orbitals with a given (n, l) is $2(2\text{l} + 1)$,
since there are two spin states for given m-value. When all these $2(2\text{l} + 1)$ states are
filled, we speak of the closing of a shell. The charge density for a closed shell has the
form

\begin{equation}
-\text{e} \sum_{\text{m}=-1}^{\text{l}} |\text{R}_{\text{nl}}(\text{r})|^{2}|\text{Y}_{\text{lm}}(\theta, \phi)|^{2} \tag{14A-13}
\end{equation}

and this is spherically symmetric because of the property of spherical harmonics that

\begin{equation}
\sum_{\text{m}=-1}^{\text{l}} |\text{Y}_{\text{lm}}(\theta, \phi)|^{2} = \frac{2\text{l} + 1}{4\pi} \tag{14A-14}
\end{equation}
