  "base_latex_md": "W-60 \quad Supplement 14-B \quad The Building-Up Principle

\begin{figure}[h!]
\centering
%% INSERT IMAGE HERE
\caption{A diagram showing five energy levels for $Mn$ with single upward arrows in each level, labeled $2, 1, 0, -1, -2$ from top to bottom.}
\label{fig:hunds_rules}
\end{figure}

Figure 14B-3 Application of Hund's rules to atom with five valence electrons in d-state.

For manganese the shelves have $\text{L}_\text{z} = 2, 1, 0, -1, -2$, as shown in Fig. 14B-3. There are five electrons, and thus each of the spaces is filled by one. With the spins parallel we get $\text{S}_\text{z} = 5/2$, which implies that $\text{S} = 5/2$. The total value of $\text{L}_\text{z} = 0$, and thus we have an S-state. This means that the ground state is an $^6\text{S}_{5/2}$ state.

Limitations of space prevent us from a more detailed discussion of the periodic table. A few additional comments are, however, in order.

\begin{enumerate}
    \item[(a)] There is nothing in atomic structure that limits the number of elements. The reason that atoms with $Z \ge 100$ do not occur naturally is that heavy nuclei undergo spontaneous fission. If new, superheavy (metastable) nuclei are ever discovered, there will presumably exist corresponding atoms, and it is expected that their structure will conform to the prediction of the building-up approach outlined in this supplement.
    \item[(b)] The ionization energies all lie in the vicinity of 5--15 eV. The reason is that in spite of the increasing number of electrons, the outermost electrons "see" a charge that lies in the range $Z = 1\text{--}2$. In addition, because of the departures from a point charge distribution, the dependence of the energy is no longer of the $1/\text{n}^2$ form. Consequently, the wave functions of the outermost electrons do not extend much further than that of the electron in the hydrogen atom. Atoms are more or less the same size!
    \item[(c)] We went to a great deal of trouble to specify the S, L, and J quantum numbers of the ground states of the various elements. The reason for doing this is that in spectroscopy, the quantum numbers are of particular interest because of the selection rules
    \begin{align}
        \Delta \text{S} &= 0 \nonumber \\
        \Delta \text{L} &= \pm 1 \tag{14B-2} \\
        \Delta \text{J} &= 0, \pm 1 \quad \quad (\text{no } 0 - 0) \nonumber
    \end{align}
    that will be derived later, and that may then be used to determine the quantum numbers of the excited states. The spectroscopy of atoms, once we get beyond hydrogen and helium, is very complicated. Consider, as a relatively simple example, the first few states of carbon, which are formed from different configurations of the two electrons that lie outside the closed shell in the $(2\text{p})^2$ orbitals. As already pointed out, the possible states are $^1\text{S}_0$, $^3\text{P}_{2, 1, 0}$, and $^1\text{D}_2$. The $^3\text{P}_0$ state lies lowest, but the other states are still there. The first excited states may be described by the orbitals $(2\text{p})(3\text{s})$. Here $\text{S} = 0$ or 1, but $\text{L} = 1$ only. Since the n-values are different, the exclusion principle does not restrict the states in any way, and all of the states $^1\text{P}_1$, $^3\text{P}_{2, 1, 0}$ are possible, while the excited states that
\end{enumerate}",
  "annotations_metadata": []
}

