  "base_latex_md": "A Brief Discussion of Molecules W-65

\begin{figure}[h!]
\centering
%% INSERT IMAGE HERE
\caption{Figure 14C-1 Coordinate labels in the discussion of the $\text{H}_2$ molecule. The image shows a geometric diagram representing two protons $A$ and $B$ on an axis $z$ separated by distance $R_{AB}$, and two electrons $1$ and $2$. Distances between the electrons and protons are labeled $r_{1A}, r_{2A}, r_{1B}, r_{2B}$, and the distance between the two electrons is $r_{12}$.}
\label{fig:description}
\end{figure}

The electron spin state is a singlet, since the spatial part of the wave function is taken to be symmetric. In this trial wave function, each electron is associated with both protons; that is, the trial wave function is said to be a product of molecular orbitals. The description in terms of molecular orbitals is sometimes called the MO method.

The calculation of $\langle \psi_g | \text{H} | \psi_g \rangle$ yields

\begin{align}
\Bigg\langle \psi_g \Bigg| \Bigg(\tilde{\text{H}}_1 - \frac{e^2}{4\pi\varepsilon_0 \text{R}_{\text{AB}}}\Bigg) &+ \Bigg(\tilde{\text{H}}_2 - \frac{e^2}{4\pi\varepsilon_0 \text{R}_{\text{AB}}}\Bigg) + \frac{e^2}{4\pi\varepsilon_0 \text{r}_{12}} + \frac{e^2}{4\pi\varepsilon_0 \text{R}_{\text{AB}}} \Bigg| \psi_g \Bigg\rangle \nonumber \\
&= \text{E}(\text{R}_{\text{AB}}) + \text{E}(\text{R}_{\text{AB}}) + \Bigg\langle \psi_g \Bigg| \frac{e^2}{4\pi\varepsilon_0 \text{r}_{12}} \Bigg| \psi_g \Bigg\rangle - \frac{e^2}{4\pi\varepsilon_0 \text{R}_{\text{AB}}} \tag{14C-5} \\
&= 2\text{E}(\text{R}_{\text{AB}}) - \frac{e^2}{4\pi\varepsilon_0 \text{R}_{\text{AB}}} + \Bigg\langle \psi_g \Bigg| \frac{e^2}{4\pi\varepsilon_0 \text{r}_{12}} \Bigg| \psi_g \Bigg\rangle \nonumber
\end{align}

where $\text{E}(\text{R}_{\text{AB}})$ is the energy of the $\text{H}_2^+$ molecule calculated in (14.56). The first-order electron--electron repulsion contribution can also be calculated, and when the total energy so computed is minimized with respect to the separation $\text{R}_{\text{AB}}$, it is found that the binding energy and internuclear separation are given by

\begin{align*}
\text{E}_{\text{b}} &= -2.68 \text{ eV} \\
\text{R} &= 0.85 \text{ \AA}
\end{align*}

The experimental values are

\begin{align*}
\text{E}_{\text{b}} &= -4.75 \text{ eV} \\
\text{R} &= 0.74 \text{ \AA}
\end{align*}

Evidently the approximation is not a very good one. We noted in our discussion of the $\text{H}_2^+$ molecule that the trial wave functions (the MO's) are inaccurate for small proton--proton separations, and the fact that the MO's are too spread out in space shows up in the numbers above. The trial wave function also has some undesirable features for large $\text{R}_{\text{AB}}$. The product in (14C-4) may be rewritten in the form

\begin{align}
[\psi_{\text{A}}(\text{r}_1) + \psi_{\text{B}}(\text{r}_1)][\psi_{\text{A}}(\text{r}_2) + \psi_{\text{B}}(\text{r}_2)] \nonumber \\
= [\psi_{\text{A}}(\text{r}_1)\psi_{\text{A}}(\text{r}_2) &+ \psi_{\text{B}}(\text{r}_1)\psi_{\text{B}}(\text{r}_2)] + [\psi_{\text{A}}(\text{r}_1)\psi_{\text{B}}(\text{r}_2) + \psi_{\text{A}}(\text{r}_2)\psi_{\text{B}}(\text{r}_1)] \tag{14C-6}
\end{align}

The first term is called an ``ionic'' term, since it describes both electrons bound to one proton or the other. The second term, the ``covalent'' term, is a description in terms of linear combinations of atomic orbitals (LCAO). Our trial wave function thus implies, since the two terms enter with equal weight, that for large $\text{R}_{\text{AB}}$ the molecule is as likely to dissociate into the ions $\text{H}^+$ and $\text{H}^-$ as it is into two hydrogen atoms, and this is patently false.",
  "annotations_metadata": []
}

