  "base_latex_md": "W-66 Supplement 14-C A Brief Discussion of Molecules

\section*{The Valence Bond Method}

The last difficulty can be avoided with the use of the valence bond (also called Heitler-London) method, in which linear combinations of atomic orbitals are used. The singlet wave function used as a trial wave function in the variational principle is taken to be

\begin{equation}
\psi(r_1, r_2) = \left\{ \frac{1}{2[1 + S^2(R_{AB})]} \right\}^{1/2} [\psi_A(r_1)\psi_B(r_2) + \psi_A(r_2)\psi_B(r_1)] \text{ X}_{\text{singlet}} \quad (14\text{C-}7)
\end{equation}

where, as before, the $\psi_A(r_i)$ are hydrogenic wave functions for the ith electron about proton A. We could, in principle, add a triplet term to our variational trial wave function. However, a triplet wave function must be spatially antisymmetric and yields low probability for the electrons being located in the region between the protons. We saw in our discussion of the $\text{H}_2^+$ molecule that just this configuration led to the lowest energy. Although it is not immediately obvious that the attraction is still largest in this configuration when there are two electrons that repel each other in the system, it is in fact so. The results of a variational calculation with the VB trial wave function are

\begin{align*}
E_b &= -3.14 \text{ eV} \\
R &= 0.87 \text{ \AA}
\end{align*}

This is not a significant improvement over the MO results, for the simple reason that the inadequacy of the trial wave functions for small $R_{AB}$ carries more weight. There should be no question about the quantitative successes of quantum mechanics in molecular physics. More sophisticated trial wave functions have to be used; for example, a 50-term trial wave function yields complete agreement with observations for the $\text{H}_2$ molecule, but it does not, as the MO and VB functions do, give us something of a qualitative feeling of what goes on between the atoms. In what follows, we will explore the relevance of these approaches to a qualitative understanding of some aspects of chemistry.

The expectation value of H for the $\text{H}_2$ molecule in the VB approach has the following schematic form:

\begin{align*}
\langle \psi|\text{H}|\psi \rangle &= \frac{1}{2(1 + S^2)} \langle \psi_{A1}\psi_{B2} + \psi_{A2}\psi_{B1}|\text{H}|\psi_{A1}\psi_{B2} + \psi_{A2}\psi_{B1} \rangle \\
&= \frac{1}{1 + S^2} \Bigg\langle \psi_{A1}\psi_{B2}\Bigg|\text{T}_1 + \text{T}_2 - \frac{e^2}{4\pi\varepsilon_0 r_{A1}} - \frac{e^2}{4\pi\varepsilon_0 r_{A2}} - \frac{e^2}{4\pi\varepsilon_0 r_{B1}} - \frac{e^2}{4\pi\varepsilon_0 r_{B2}} \\
&\quad + \frac{e^2}{4\pi\varepsilon_0 r_{12}} + \frac{e^2}{4\pi\varepsilon_0 \text{R}_{AB}}\Bigg|\psi_{A1}\psi_{B2} + \psi_{A2}\psi_{B1} \Bigg\rangle
\end{align*}

where $\text{T}_i$ is the kinetic energy of the ith electron, and since

\begin{equation*}
\left(\text{T}_1 - \frac{e^2}{4\pi\varepsilon_0 r_{A1}}\right)\psi_{A1} = \text{E}_1\psi_{A1}
\end{equation*}

\begin{align*}
\langle \psi|\text{H}|\psi \rangle &= \frac{1}{2(1 + S^2)} \langle \psi_{A1}\psi_{B2} + \psi_{A2}\psi_{B1}|\text{H}|\psi_{A1}\psi_{B2} + \psi_{A2}\psi_{B1} \rangle \\
&= \frac{1}{1 + S^2} \Bigg\langle \psi_{A1}\psi_{B2}\Bigg|2\text{E}_1 - \frac{e^2}{4\pi\varepsilon_0 r_{B1}} - \frac{e^2}{4\pi\varepsilon_0 r_{A2}} + \frac{e^2}{4\pi\varepsilon_0 r_{12}} + \frac{e^2}{4\pi\varepsilon_0 \text{R}_{AB}}\Bigg|\psi_{A1}\psi_{B2} \Bigg\rangle \\
&\quad + \left\langle \psi_{A1}\psi_{B2}\Bigg|2\text{E}_1 - \frac{e^2}{4\pi\varepsilon_0 r_{B2}} - \frac{e^2}{4\pi\varepsilon_0 r_{A1}} + \frac{e^2}{4\pi\varepsilon_0 r_{12}} + \frac{e^2}{4\pi\varepsilon_0 \text{R}_{AB}}\Bigg|\psi_{A2}\psi_{B1} \right\rangle
\end{align*}",
  "annotations_metadata": []
}

