For the discussion of systems involving only two or three levels, it is particularly conve-
nient to use a description of the time evolution of the system that lies between the
Schrödinger picture and the Heisenberg picture, both of which were discussed in Chapter 6.
Let us start with the Schrödinger equation, which reads
\begin{equation}
\frac{\mathrm{d}}{\mathrm{d}t} |\psi(t)\rangle = -\frac{\mathrm{i}}{\hbar} \mathrm{H} |\psi(t)\rangle = -\frac{\mathrm{i}}{\hbar} (\mathrm{H}_0 + \mathrm{H}_1)|\psi(t)\rangle \tag{15B-1}
\end{equation}
We can write this in the form
\begin{equation}
|\psi(t)\rangle = \mathrm{U}(t)|\psi(0)\rangle \tag{15B-2}
\end{equation}
where
\begin{equation}
\frac{\mathrm{d}}{\mathrm{d}t} \mathrm{U}(t) = -\frac{\mathrm{i}}{\hbar} (\mathrm{H}_0 + \mathrm{H}_1)\mathrm{U}(t) \tag{15B-3}
\end{equation}
The initial condition is $\mathrm{U}(0) = 1$.

The procedure calls for the definition of a new state vector $|\psi_{\mathrm{I}}(t)\rangle$ defined by
\begin{equation}
|\psi_{\mathrm{I}}(t)\rangle = \mathrm{e}^{\mathrm{i}\mathrm{H}_0t/\hbar}|\psi(t)\rangle \tag{15B-4}
\end{equation}
It follows that
\begin{align*}
\frac{\mathrm{d}}{\mathrm{d}t} |\psi_{\mathrm{I}}(t)\rangle &= \frac{\mathrm{i}}{\hbar} \mathrm{H}_0|\psi_{\mathrm{I}}(t)\rangle + \mathrm{e}^{\mathrm{i}\mathrm{H}_0t/\hbar}\left(-\frac{\mathrm{i}}{\hbar}\right)(\mathrm{H}_0 + \mathrm{H}_1)|\psi(t)\rangle \\
&= \mathrm{e}^{\mathrm{i}\mathrm{H}_0t/\hbar}\left(-\frac{\mathrm{i}}{\hbar}\right)\mathrm{H}_1\mathrm{e}^{-\mathrm{i}\mathrm{H}_0t/\hbar}|\psi_{\mathrm{I}}(t)\rangle
\end{align*}
If we now define
\begin{equation}
\mathrm{V}(t) = \mathrm{e}^{\mathrm{i}\mathrm{H}_0t/\hbar}\mathrm{H}_1\mathrm{e}^{-\mathrm{i}\mathrm{H}_0t/\hbar} \tag{15B-5}
\end{equation}
we end up with the equation
\begin{equation}
\frac{\mathrm{d}}{\mathrm{d}t} |\psi_{\mathrm{I}}(t)\rangle = \left(-\frac{\mathrm{i}}{\hbar}\right)\mathrm{V}(t)|\psi_{\mathrm{I}}(t)\rangle \tag{15B-6}
\end{equation}
Solving this equation is not trivial, and in general the best one can do is to find a solution
in terms of a power series in V(t). The formal procedure for solving this in a way that in-
corporates the initial condition
\begin{equation}
|\psi_{\mathrm{I}}(0)\rangle = |\phi\rangle \tag{15B-7}
\end{equation}
is to write
\begin{equation}
|\psi_{\mathrm{I}}(t)\rangle = \mathrm{U}_{\mathrm{I}}(t)|\phi\rangle \tag{15B-8}
\end{equation}

