\section*{Spin and Intensity Rules}

The inclusion of spin does not change things very much. It is true that the initial states and the final states can each be in an ``up'' or ``down'' spin state, but since the interaction in atomic transitions is spin independent, only ``up'' $\rightarrow$ ``up'' and ``down'' $\rightarrow$ ``down'' transitions are allowed. Hence the transition rates will not only be independent of $\text{m}_l$ (as we saw in Chapter 17) but also of $\text{m}_s$, and hence, $\text{m}_j$. With the inclusion of spin-orbit coupling, there will be small (on the scale of the $2\text{p} - 1\text{s}$ energy difference) level splittings. For example, the $\text{n} = 1$ and $\text{n} = 2$ level structure is changed, as shown in Fig. 17-A1. The spectral line corresponding to the transition $2\text{p} \rightarrow 1\text{s}$ is split into two lines, $2^2\text{P}_{3/2} \rightarrow 1^2\text{S}_{1/2}$ and $2^2\text{P}_{1/2} \rightarrow 1^2\text{S}_{1/2}$. For the split states, the radial integral and the phase space are almost unchanged, and hence the ratio of the intensity of the two lines can be determined from the angular parts of the integral alone---that is, purely from angular momentum considerations.

The following table lists the wave functions for the states in question.

\begin{table}[h!]
\centering
\begin{tabular}{lrlc}
\hline
& & \multicolumn{1}{c}{Odd Parity} & Even Parity \\
$\text{J}$ & $\text{m}_j$ & \multicolumn{1}{c}{$1 = 1$} & $1 = 0$ \\
\hline
$3/2$ & $3/2$ & $\text{Y}_{11}\chi_{+}$ & --- \\
$3/2$ & $1/2$ & $\sqrt{2/3} \ \text{Y}_{10}\chi_{+} + \sqrt{1/3} \ \text{Y}_{11}\chi_{-}$ & --- \\
$3/2$ & $-1/2$ & $\sqrt{1/3} \ \text{Y}_{1,-1}\chi_{+} + \sqrt{2/3} \ \text{Y}_{10}\chi_{-}$ & --- \\
$3/2$ & $-3/2$ & $\text{Y}_{1,-1}\chi_{-}$ & --- \\
$1/2$ & $1/2$ & $\sqrt{1/3} \ \text{Y}_{10}\chi_{+} - \sqrt{2/3} \ \text{Y}_{11}\chi_{-}$ & $\text{Y}_{00}\chi_{+}$ \\
$1/2$ & $-1/2$ & $\sqrt{2/3} \ \text{Y}_{1,-1}\chi_{+} - \sqrt{1/3} \ \text{Y}_{10}\chi_{-}$ & $\text{Y}_{00}\chi_{-}$ \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

