  "base_latex_md": "19-4 Scattering of Identical Particles \quad \textbf{307}

In the scattering of two unpolarized protons, all spin states are equally likely, and thus the
probability of finding the two protons in a triplet state is three times as large as finding
them in a singlet state, so that

\begin{align}
\frac{d\sigma}{d\Omega} &= \frac{3}{4}\frac{d\sigma_{t}}{d\Omega} + \frac{1}{4}\frac{d\sigma_{s}}{d\Omega} \nonumber \\
&= \frac{3}{4}|f(\theta) - f(\pi - \theta)|^2 + \frac{1}{4}|f(\theta) + f(\pi - \theta)|^2 \tag{19-97} \\
&= |f(\theta)|^2 + |f(\pi - \theta)|^2 - \frac{1}{2}[f(\theta)f^*(\pi - \theta) + f^*(\theta)f(\pi - \theta)] \nonumber
\end{align}

For proton–proton scattering as well as for $\alpha$–$\alpha$ scattering, the basic amplitude $f(\theta)$ is the sum
of a nuclear term (if the energies are not too low) and a Coulomb term. Whether the identical
particles are bosons or fermions, there is a symmetry under the interchange $\theta \rightarrow \pi - \theta$.

\section*{Scattering by Atoms on a Lattice}

Symmetry considerations also play a role in the scattering of particles by a crystal lattice.
If we ignore spin, so that we do not have to worry whether the electron does or does not
flip its spin (“up” $\rightarrow$ “down” or vice versa), then at low energies, the scattering amplitude
$f(\theta)$ is independent of angle ($S$-wave scattering), and the solution of the Schr\"odinger equa
tion by a single atom located at the lattice point $\mathbf{a}_i$ has the asymptotic form

\begin{equation}
\psi(\mathbf{r}) \sim e^{i\mathbf{k} \cdot (\mathbf{r} - \mathbf{a}_i)} + f\frac{e^{ik|\mathbf{r} - \mathbf{a}_i|}}{|\mathbf{r} - \mathbf{a}_i|} \tag{19-98}
\end{equation}

Now

\begin{align}
k|\mathbf{r} - \mathbf{a}_i| &= k(r^2 - 2\mathbf{r} \cdot \mathbf{a}_i + a_i^2)^{1/2} \nonumber \\
&\cong kr\left(1 - \frac{2\mathbf{r} \cdot \mathbf{a}_i}{r^2}\right)^{1/2} \tag{19-99} \\
&\cong kr - k\hat{\mathbf{r}} \cdot \mathbf{a}_i \nonumber
\end{align}

and since $k\hat{\mathbf{r}}$, is a vector of magnitude $k$ and it points in the direction $\mathbf{r}$, the point of obser
vation, it is the final momentum $\mathbf{k}'$. If we divide out the phase factor $e^{-i\mathbf{k} \cdot \mathbf{a}_i}$, the wave func
tion has the asymptotic form

\begin{equation}
\psi \sim e^{i\mathbf{k} \cdot \mathbf{r}} + f\, e^{-i\mathbf{k}' \cdot \mathbf{a}_i}\, e^{i\mathbf{k} \cdot \mathbf{a}_i}\, \frac{e^{ikr}}{r} + O\left(\frac{1}{r^2}\right) \tag{19-100}
\end{equation}

so that the scattering amplitude is

\begin{equation}
f(\theta) = f\, e^{-i\Delta \cdot \mathbf{a}_i} \qquad \Delta = \mathbf{k}' - \mathbf{k} \tag{19-101}
\end{equation}

The total amplitude is the sum of all individual scattering amplitudes when we have a sit
uation in which we cannot tell which atom in the crystal did the scattering. This is indeed
the case for elastic low-energy scattering when recoil is not observed and spins are not
measured. Thus, for the \textit{coherent} process we have

\begin{equation}
\frac{d\sigma}{d\Omega} = \left| f \sum_{\text{atoms}} e^{-i\Delta \cdot \mathbf{a}_i} \right|^2 \tag{19-102}
\end{equation}

If we have a simple cubic array of lattice points, such that

\begin{equation}
\mathbf{a}_i = a(n_x\hat{\mathbf{i}}_x + n_y\hat{\mathbf{i}}_y + n_z\hat{\mathbf{i}}_z) \qquad -N \leq n_x, n_y, n_z \leq N \tag{19-103}
\end{equation}

(spacings are integral multiples of $a$ in all directions), then

\begin{equation*}
\sum e^{-i\Delta \cdot \mathbf{a}_i} = \sum_{n_x=-N}^{N}\sum_{n_y=-N}^{N}\sum_{n_z=-N}^{N} e^{-ia\Delta_x n_x}\, e^{-ia\Delta_y n_y}\, e^{-ia\Delta_z n_z}
