\section*{The Creation of GHZ States}

The apparatus developed by Bouwmeester et al.$^1$ is shown in Fig. 20B-1. A short pulse of ultraviolet light passes through a nonlinear crystal, creating two pairs of photons, close enough in time that in terms of the counter time resolution they appear simultaneously. The photons move along paths a and b, and each pair of photons is entangled in that the polarization states are perpendicular to each other (the notation H and V is used for horizontal and vertical in the plane perpendicular to the propagation of the photons), in such a way that each pair may be described by the state

\begin{equation}
\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}} \left( |\text{H}\rangle_{\text{a}} |\text{V}\rangle_{\text{b}} - |\text{V}\rangle_{\text{a}} |\text{H}\rangle_{\text{b}} \right) \tag{20B-1}
\end{equation}

The arm a leads to a polarizing beam-splitter. It acts to transmit H photons, which then continue to a detector, labeled T. This means that

\begin{equation}
|\text{H}\rangle_{\text{a}} \rightarrow |\text{H}\rangle_{\text{T}} \tag{20B-2}
\end{equation}

The V photons are reflected. They move along the arm $\alpha$ and are made to pass through a $\lambda/2$ plate, which rotates their polarization (“V”) through $45^{\circ}$. At the polarizing beam splitter, the V-component is deflected to counter $\text{D}_1$, while the H-component goes on to counter $\text{D}_2$. This means that

\begin{equation}
|\text{V}\rangle_{\text{a}} \rightarrow \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}} \left( |\text{V}\rangle_1 + |\text{H}\rangle_2 \right) \tag{20B-3}
\end{equation}

The photons going along the arm b are directed to a polarization-independent beam-splitter, so that the photons reaching BS have a 50\% chance of passing through to detector $\text{D}_3$ and a 50\% chance of being deflected along the arm $\beta$. The photons moving along $\beta$ strike the polarizing beam-splitter. The H photons go on to the detector $\text{D}_1$, while the V photons that continue along the arm $\beta$ and go on to the detector $\text{D}_2$. This implies that

\begin{equation}
|\text{H}\rangle_{\text{b}} \rightarrow \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}} \left( |\text{H}\rangle_3 + |\text{H}\rangle_1 \right) \tag{20B-4}
\end{equation}

while

\begin{equation}
|\text{V}\rangle_{\text{b}} \rightarrow \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}} \left( |\text{V}\rangle_3 + |\text{V}\rangle_2 \right) \tag{20B-5}
\end{equation}

