We consider a potential $V(x)$ whose spatial behavior is given by $\delta(x)$. Since $\delta(x)$ has the dimensions of a reciprocal length, it is convenient to write for the attractive potential.

\begin{equation}
V(x) = -\frac{\hbar^2 \lambda}{2ma} \delta(x)
\end{equation}

We have introduced $a$, an arbitrary quantity with dimensions of a length, so that $\lambda$ is a dimensionless quantity that we use to characterize the strength of the potential. The equation to be solved is, when $E < 0$,

\begin{equation}
\frac{d^2u(x)}{dx^2} - \kappa^2u(x) = -\frac{\lambda}{a} \delta(x) u(x)
\end{equation}

where $\kappa^2 = 2m|E|/\hbar^2$. The solution everywhere, except at $x = 0$, must satisfy the equation $d^2u/dx^2 - \kappa^2u = 0$, and if it is to vanish at $x \to \pm\infty$, we must have

\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
u(x) &= e^{-\kappa x} \quad x > 0 \\
&= e^{\kappa x} \quad x < 0
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}

The coefficients in front are the same (and here chosen to be unity—we can normalize afterwards) because of the continuity of the wave function. The derivative of the wave function is no longer continuous. As argued before (Eq. 4-13), we have

\begin{equation}
\left(\frac{du}{dx}\right)_{x=0+} - \left(\frac{du}{dx}\right)_{x=0-} = -\frac{\lambda}{a}u(0)
\end{equation}

The last relation gives the eigenvalue condition

\[ -\kappa - \kappa = -\frac{\lambda}{a} \]

that is,

\begin{equation}
\kappa = \frac{\lambda}{2a}
\end{equation}

