  "base_latex_md": "\section*{Supplement \textbf{7-A}}

\vspace{2em}
\Huge{Rotational Invariance}
\normalsize
\vspace{2em}

In this supplement we show that the assumption of a central potential implies the conservation of angular momentum. We make use of invariance under rotations. The kinetic energy, which involves $\mathrm{p}^2$, is independent of the direction in which $\mathrm{p}$ points. The central potential $\mathrm{V}(\mathrm{r})$ is also invariant under rotations. We show that this invariance implies the conservation of angular momentum.

\subsection*{INVARIANCE UNDER ROTATIONS ABOUT THE z-AXIS}

Consider the special case of a rotation through an angle $\theta$ about the z-axis: With
\begin{align}
\mathrm{x}' &= \mathrm{x}\ \cos\ \theta - \mathrm{y}\ \sin\ \theta \nonumber \\
\mathrm{y}' &= \mathrm{x}\ \sin\ \theta + \mathrm{y}\ \cos\ \theta \tag{7A-1}
\end{align}
it is easy to see that
\begin{equation}
\mathrm{r}' = ({\mathrm{x}'}^2 + {\mathrm{y}'}^2 + {\mathrm{z}'}^2)^{1/2} = (\mathrm{x}^2 + \mathrm{y}^2 + \mathrm{z}^2)^{1/2} = \mathrm{r} \tag{7A-2}
\end{equation}
and
\begin{align}
\left(\frac{\partial}{\partial \mathrm{x}'}\right)^2 + \left(\frac{\partial}{\partial \mathrm{y}'}\right)^2 &= \left( \cos\ \theta \frac{\partial}{\partial \mathrm{x}} - \sin\ \theta \frac{\partial}{\partial \mathrm{y}} \right)^2 + \left( \sin\ \theta \frac{\partial}{\partial \mathrm{x}} + \cos\ \theta \frac{\partial}{\partial \mathrm{y}} \right)^2 \nonumber \\
&= \left(\frac{\partial}{\partial \mathrm{x}}\right)^2 + \left(\frac{\partial}{\partial \mathrm{y}}\right)^2 \tag{7A-3}
\end{align}

Since the Hamiltonian has an invariance property, we expect a conservation law, as we saw in the case of parity. To identify the operators that commute with $\mathrm{H}$, let us consider an infinitesimal rotation about the z-axis. Keeping terms of order $\theta$ only so that
\begin{align}
\mathrm{x}' &= \mathrm{x} - \theta \mathrm{y} \nonumber \\
\mathrm{y}' &= \mathrm{y} + \theta \mathrm{x} \tag{7A-4}
\end{align}
we require that
\begin{equation}
\mathrm{H} \mathrm{u}_\mathrm{E}(\mathrm{x} - \theta \mathrm{y}, \mathrm{y} + \theta \mathrm{x}, \mathrm{z}) = \mathrm{E} \mathrm{u}_\mathrm{E}(\mathrm{x} - \theta \mathrm{y}, \mathrm{y} + \theta \mathrm{x}, \mathrm{z}) \tag{7A-5}
\end{equation}
If we expand this to first order in $\theta$ and subtract from it
\begin{equation}
\mathrm{H} \mathrm{u}_\mathrm{E}(\mathrm{x}, \mathrm{y}, \mathrm{z}) = \mathrm{E} \mathrm{u}_\mathrm{E}(\mathrm{x}, \mathrm{y}, \mathrm{z}) \tag{7A-6}
\end{equation}
we obtain from the term linear in $\theta$
\begin{equation}
\mathrm{H} \! \left( \mathrm{x} \frac{\partial}{\partial \mathrm{y}} - \mathrm{y} \frac{\partial}{\partial \mathrm{x}} \right) \! \mathrm{u}_\mathrm{E}(\mathrm{x}, \mathrm{y}, \mathrm{z}) = \mathrm{E} \! \left( \mathrm{x} \frac{\partial}{\partial \mathrm{y}} - \mathrm{y} \frac{\partial}{\partial \mathrm{x}} \right) \! \mathrm{u}_\mathrm{E}(\mathrm{x}, \mathrm{y}, \mathrm{z}) \tag{7A-7}
\end{equation}

