\section*{The Schr\"odinger Equation in Three Dimensions and the Hydrogen Atom}

\textbf{T}he energy operator $H$ in three dimensions is a simple generalization of the one-dimensional energy. The Schr\"odinger equation takes the form
\begin{equation}
H\psi(\textbf{r}) = \left(\frac{\textbf{p}^2}{2\mu} + V(\textbf{r})\right)\psi(\textbf{r}) = E\psi(\textbf{r})
\label{8-1}
\end{equation}
Since the letter $m$ (usually reserved for the mass) will be used to denote the $z$-component of the angular momentum, we use $\mu$ for the mass.\footnotemark[1]

