{
  "base_latex_md": "The Square Well, Continuum Solutions \quad W-39

wave, with the constraint that the magnitude of the incoming flux and the outgoing flux be
equal. Thus if the asymptotic solution has the form

\begin{equation}
R_l(kr) \to -\frac{1}{2ikr}(e^{-i(kr-l\pi/2)} - S_l(k)e^{i(kr-l\pi/2)})
\tag{8B-4}
\end{equation}

then it is required that

\begin{equation}
|S_l(k)|^2 = 1
\tag{8B-5}
\end{equation}

We write $S_l(k)$ in the standard form

\begin{equation}
S_l(k) = e^{2i\delta_l(k)}
\tag{8B-6}
\end{equation}

The real function $\delta_l(k)$ is called the phase shift, because the asymptotic form of the radial
function (8B-4) may be rewritten in the form

\begin{equation}
R_l(kr) \to e^{i\delta_l(k)}\frac{\sin(kr - l\pi/2 + \delta_l(k))}{kr}
\tag{8B-7}
\end{equation}

Aside from the irrelevant phase factor in front, this differs from the asymptotic form of
the free-particle solution only by a shift in phase of the argument.

We note parenthetically that with a solution that has a $1/r$ behavior, the flux in any di-
rection other than radial goes to zero as $1/r^2$, and we were therefore justified in only con-
sidering the radial flux at large values of r.

Let us now consider the special case of a square well. The above argument shows us
that we only need to consider the phase shift, since at large distances from the well the
only deviation from free particle behavior is the phase shift.

We again consider the well

\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
V(r) &= -V_0 \quad &\text{for } r \le a \\
&= 0 \quad &\text{for } r > a
\end{aligned}
\tag{8B-8}
\end{equation}

We again use the notation

\begin{equation}
\kappa^2 = \frac{2\mu(E + V_0)}{\hbar^2}
\tag{8B-9}
\end{equation}

Now the solution for $r \le a$ must be regular at the origin, so that it has the form

\begin{equation}
R_l(r) = Aj_l(\kappa r) \quad r \le a
\tag{8B-10}
\end{equation}

The solution for $r > a$ will contain an irregular part, so that we have

\begin{equation}
R_l(r) = Bj_l(kr) + Cn_l(kr) \quad r \ge a
\tag{8B-11}
\end{equation}

The matching of $\frac{1}{R_l(r)}\frac{dR_l(r)}{dr}$ at $r = a$ yields an expression

\begin{equation}
\kappa \left[ \frac{dj_l(\rho)/d\rho}{j_l(\rho)} \right]_{\rho=\kappa a} = k \left[ \frac{Bdj_l/d\rho + Cdn_l/d\rho}{Bj_l(\rho) + Cn_l(\rho)} \right]_{\rho=ka}
\tag{8B-12}
\end{equation}

from which the ratio C/B can be calculated. The ratio can be related to the phase shift. We
do this by looking at the asymptotic form of the larger r solution, which has the form

\[
R_l(r) \to B\frac{\sin(kr - l\pi/2)}{kr} - C\frac{\cos(kr - l\pi/2)}{kr}
\]",
