\section*{The Emergence\\ of Quantum Physics}

\textbf{Q}\textit{uantum theory} grew out of an interplay of ground-breaking experiments and radical theoretical proposals that were not based on accepted classical physics. Our objective in this chapter is to summarize briefly the critical events that took place between 1900 and 1925, when quantum mechanics was created. We shall be very brief in our coverage, since these matters are discussed in much more detail in textbooks on modern physics.\footnote{See, for example, \textit{Modern Physics} by J. Bernstein, P. M. Fishbane, and S. Gasiorowicz, Prentice Hall, Englewood Cliffs, N.J., 2000.}

\section{1-1 BLACKBODY RADIATION}

The study of radiation emitted by a heated body in equilibrium, initiated by the German physicist Gustav Kirchhoff in 1859, led to the recognition that the intensity of radiation emitted by a totally absorbing surface (a \textit{black body})---in practice a small hole in a heated enclosure---was a universal function of the temperature $T$ and the wavelength $\lambda$ (or frequency $\nu$) of the radiation under consideration. For a cavity, the energy emitted per unit area, per unit time, in the form of this \textit{blackbody radiation} in the wavelength range $\lambda$ to $\lambda + d\lambda$, $E(\lambda, T)d\lambda$, is related to the energy density of radiation $w(\lambda, T)d\lambda$ inside the cavity

\begin{equation}
w(\lambda, T) = \frac{4E(\lambda, T)}{c}
\tag{1-1}
\end{equation}

where $c$ is the speed of light. Wilhelm Wien showed in 1894 that classical thermodynamics led to the requirement that $w(\lambda, T)$ had to have the form

\begin{equation}
w(\lambda, T) = \frac{f(\lambda T)}{\lambda^5}
\tag{1-2}
\end{equation}

a result borne out by measurements. (See Fig. 1-1.) If the function $f(x)$ has a maximum, then at that maximum $\lambda T = x_{\text{max}}$. Experiments determined that

\begin{equation}
\lambda_{\text{max}} = \frac{b}{T}
\tag{1-3}
\end{equation}

where $b = 2.898 \times 10^{-3} \text{ m\cdot K}$. In terms of the frequency $\nu$, with $\nu = c/\lambda$, the energy density inside the cavity is

\begin{equation}
u(\nu, T) = w(\lambda, T) \left| \frac{d\lambda}{d\nu} \right| = w\left(\frac{c}{\nu}, T\right) \frac{c}{\nu^2}
\tag{1-4}
\end{equation}

\vspace{2em}
\hrule
\vspace{0.5em}
\footnotesize{${}^1$See, for example, \textit{Modern Physics} by J. Bernstein, P. M. Fishbane, and S. Gasiorowicz, Prentice Hall, Englewood Cliffs, N.J., 2000.}

\begin{flushright}
\textbf{1}
\end{flushright}",
