\huge{Einstein's Approach}

\huge{to Planck's Law}
\normalsize

\vspace{0.5cm}
In 1917 Albert Einstein wrote a remarkable paper in which he used classical statistical mechanics and elements of the old Bohr theory to derive the Planck distribution and to relate spontaneous emission, as it appears in the Bohr theory to induced emission and absorption.

We start with radiation in a cavity at temperature T. Assume that the walls of the cavity consist of atoms that can be in a variety of stationary states. These are labeled by energies $\text{E}_\text{n}$, and furthermore we allow for degeneracy$\text{\textemdash}$that is, the possibility that each energy level may accommodate $\text{g}_\text{n}$ states. According to classical statistical mechanics (Boltzmann distribution), the ratio of the number of atoms in the state m to those in the state n is given by

\begin{equation}
\frac{\text{N}_\text{m}}{\text{N}_\text{n}} = \frac{\text{g}_\text{m}\text{e}^{-\text{E}_\text{m}/\text{kT}}}{\text{g}_\text{n}\text{e}^{-\text{E}_\text{n}/\text{kT}}} = \frac{\text{g}_\text{m}}{\text{g}_\text{n}}\text{e}^{-(\text{E}_\text{m}-\text{E}_\text{n})/\text{kT}} \tag{1A-1}
\end{equation}

Let us now consider a pair of levels of energies $\text{E}_1$ and $\text{E}_0$ with $\text{E}_1 > \text{E}_0$ (Fig. 1A-1). The number of transitions from the upper to the lower state per second$\text{\textemdash}$that is, the rate of transitions$\text{\textemdash}$is denoted by $\text{R}_{10}$. The rate of transitions from the lower to the upper level is $\text{R}_{01}$. The number of transitions from the lower level to the upper level must be proportional to the number of atoms in the lower state $\text{N}_0$, as well as to the intensity of the radiation in the cavity. For frequency $\nu$ and temperature T this is denoted by u($\nu$, T). We write

\begin{equation}
\text{R}_{01} = \text{N}_0\text{u}(\nu, \text{T})\text{B}_{01} \tag{1A-2}
\end{equation}

The frequency $\nu$ is the one that is absorbed in the induced absorption, and the constant $\text{B}_{01}$ is called the coefficient of induced absorption.

For the transition from the upper level to the lower level, Einstein uses the Bohr postulate, according to which spontaneous emission occurs with a rate independent of the radiation present. In addition, the presence of radiation also induces transitions to the lower level. The coefficient of induced emission is denoted by $\text{B}_{10}$. If the number of atoms in the upper state is $\text{N}_1$, then

\begin{equation}
\text{R}_{10} = \text{N}_1(\text{A}_{10} + \text{u}(\nu, \text{T})\text{B}_{10}) \tag{1A-3}
\end{equation}

with the two terms describing spontaneous and induced emission. In equilibrium there must be as many "up" transitions as "down" transitions, so that

\begin{equation}
\text{R}_{10} = \text{R}_{01} \tag{1A-4}
\end{equation}

It follows that

\begin{equation}
\frac{\text{N}_0}{\text{N}_1} = \frac{\text{A}_{10} + \text{B}_{10}\text{u}(\nu, \text{T})}{\text{B}_{01}\text{u}(\nu, \text{T})} = \frac{\text{g}_0}{\text{g}_1}\text{e}^{-(\text{E}_0-\text{E}_1)/\text{kT}} \tag{1A-5}
\end{equation}

