\hrulefill

{\huge The Fourier Integral}\\{\huge and Delta Functions}

Consider a function f(x) that is periodic, with period 2L, so that
\begin{equation}
f(x) = f(x + 2L)
\tag{2A-1}
\end{equation}
Such a function can be expanded in a Fourier series in the interval $(-L, L)$, and the series has the form
\begin{equation}
f(x) = \sum_{n=0}^\infty A_n \cos \frac{n\pi x}{L} + \sum_{n=1}^\infty B_n \sin \frac{n\pi x}{L}
\tag{2A-2}
\end{equation}
We can rewrite the series in the form
\begin{equation}
f(x) = \sum_{n=-\infty}^\infty a_n e^{in\pi x / L}
\tag{2A-3}
\end{equation}
which is certainly possible, since
\begin{align*}
\cos \frac{n\pi x}{L} &= \frac{1}{2} (e^{in\pi x / L} + e^{-in\pi x / L}) \\
\sin \frac{n\pi x}{L} &= \frac{1}{2i} (e^{in\pi x / L} - e^{-in\pi x / L})
\end{align*}
The coefficients can be determined with the help of the orthonormality relation
\begin{equation}
\frac{1}{2L} \int_{-L}^L dx \ e^{in\pi x / L} e^{-im\pi x / L} = \delta_{mn} = 
\begin{cases}
1 & \quad m = n \\
0 & \quad m \neq n
\end{cases}
\tag{2A-4}
\end{equation}
Thus
\begin{equation}
a_n = \frac{1}{2L} \int_{-L}^L dx \ f(x) e^{-in\pi x / L}
\tag{2A-5}
\end{equation}
Let us now rewrite (2A-3) by introducing $\Delta n$, the difference between two successive integers. Since this is unity, we have
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
f(x) &= \sum_{n} a_n e^{in\pi x / L} \ \Delta n \\
&= \frac{L}{\pi} \sum_{n} a_n e^{in\pi x / L} \ \frac{\pi \ \Delta n}{L}
\end{aligned}
\tag{2A-6}
\end{equation}

\vspace{2em}
