  "base_latex_md": "The Fourier Integral and Delta Functions \quad W-9

In addition to the representation (2A-15) of the delta function, there are other representations that may prove useful. We discuss several of them.

\begin{enumerate}
  \item[(a)] Consider the form (2A-15), which we write in the form
  \begin{equation}
  \delta(x) = \frac{1}{2\pi} \operatorname*{Lim}_{L\to\infty} \int\limits_{-L}^{L} dk\ e^{ikx} \tag{2A-20}
  \end{equation}
  The integral can be done, and we get
  \begin{equation}
  \begin{aligned}
  \delta(x) &= \operatorname*{Lim}_{L\to\infty} \frac{1}{2\pi} \frac{e^{iLx} - e^{-iLx}}{ix} \\
  &= \operatorname*{Lim}_{L\to\infty} \frac{\sin Lx}{\pi x}
  \end{aligned} \tag{2A-21}
  \end{equation}

  \item[(b)] Consider the function $\Delta(x, a)$ defined by
  \begin{equation}
  \begin{aligned}
  \Delta(x, a) &= 0 \qquad &x &< -a \\
  &= \frac{1}{2a} \qquad &-a &< x < a \\
  &= 0 \qquad &a &< x
  \end{aligned} \tag{2A-22}
  \end{equation}
  Then
  \begin{equation}
  \delta(x) = \operatorname*{Lim}_{a\to0} \Delta(x, a) \tag{2A-23}
  \end{equation}
  It is clear that an integral of a product of $\Delta(x, a)$ and a function $f(x)$ that is smooth near the origin will pick out the value at the origin
  \begin{align*}
  \operatorname*{Lim}_{a\to0} \int dx\ f(x)\ \Delta(x, a) &= f(0) \operatorname*{Lim}_{a\to0} \int dx\ \Delta(x, a) \\
  &= f(0)
  \end{align*}

  \item[(c)] By the same token, any peaked function, normalized to unit area under it, will approach a delta function in the limit that the width of the peak goes to zero. We will leave it to the reader to show that the following are representations of the delta function:
  \begin{equation}
  \delta(x) = \operatorname*{Lim}_{a\to0} \frac{1}{\pi} \frac{a}{x^2 + a^2} \tag{2A-24}
  \end{equation}
  and
  \begin{equation}
  \delta(x) = \operatorname*{Lim}_{a\to\infty} \frac{\alpha}{\sqrt{\pi}} e^{-\alpha^2 x^2} \tag{2A-25}
  \end{equation}

  \item[(d)] We will have occasion to deal with orthonormal polynomials, which we denote by the general symbol $P_n(x)$. These have the property that
  \begin{equation}
  \int dx\ P_m(x) P_n(x) w(x) = \delta_{mn} \tag{2A-26}
  \end{equation}
  where $w(x)$ may be unity or some simple function, called the weight function. For functions that may be expanded in a series of these orthogonal polynomials, we can write
  \begin{equation}
  f(x) = \sum_n a_n P_n(x) \tag{2A-27}
  \end{equation}
\end{enumerate}",
