If we multiply both sides by $\text{w(x)P}_\text{m}\text{(x)}$ and integrate over x, we find that

\begin{equation}
\text{a}_\text{m} = \int \text{dy w(y)f(y)P}_\text{m}\text{(y)}
\tag{2A-28}
\end{equation}

We can insert this into (2A-27) and, prepared to deal with “generalized func
tions,” we freely interchange sum and integral. We get

\begin{align}
\text{f(x)} &= \sum_\text{n} \text{P}_\text{n}\text{(x)} \int \text{dy w(y)f(y)P}_\text{n}\text{(y)} \notag \\
&= \int \text{dy f(y)}\left(\sum_\text{n} \text{P}_\text{n}\text{(x)w(y)P}_\text{n}\text{(y)}\right)
\tag{2A-29}
\end{align}

Thus we get still another representation of the delta function. Examples of the
$\text{P}_\text{n}\text{(x)}$ are Legendre polynomials, Hermite polynomials, and Laguerre polynomi
als, all of which make their appearance in quantum mechanical problems.

Since the delta function always appears multiplied by a smooth function under an in
tegral sign, we can give meaning to its derivatives. For example,

\begin{align*}
\int\limits_{-\varepsilon}^{\varepsilon} \text{dx f(x)}\frac{\text{d}}{\text{dx}}\delta\text{(x)} &= \int\limits_{-\varepsilon}^{\varepsilon} \text{dx} \frac{\text{d}}{\text{dx}}[\text{f(x)}\delta\text{(x)}] - \int\limits_{-\varepsilon}^{\varepsilon} \text{dx} \frac{\text{df(x)}}{\text{dx}}\delta\text{(x)} \\
&= -\int\limits_{-\varepsilon}^{\varepsilon} \text{dx} \frac{\text{df(x)}}{\text{dx}}\delta\text{(x)} \tag{2A-30} \\
&= -\left(\frac{\text{df}}{\text{dx}}\right)_{\text{x}=0}
\end{align*}

and so on. The delta function is an extremely useful tool, and the student will encounter it
in every part of mathematical physics.

The integral of a delta function is

\begin{align*}
\int\limits_{-\infty}^{\text{x}} \text{dy}\,\delta\text{(y - a)} &= 0 \qquad \text{x} < \text{a} \\
&= 1 \qquad \text{x} > \text{a} \tag{2A-31} \\
&\equiv \theta\text{(x - a)}
\end{align*}

which is the standard notation for this discontinuous function. Conversely, the derivative
of the so-called step function is the Dirac delta function:

\begin{equation}
\frac{\text{d}}{\text{dx}}\,\theta\text{(x - a)} = \delta\text{(x - a)}
\tag{2A-32}
\end{equation}",
