  "base_latex_md": "\section*{Supplement \text{2-B}}

\vspace{1cm}
\begin{center}
\LARGE A Brief Tutorial on Probability
\end{center}
\vspace{1cm}

In this supplement we give a brief discussion of probability. For simplicity we start with discrete events. Consider the toss of a six-faced die. If the die is not perfectly symmetric, the outcome for face n ($\text{n} = 1, 2, \dots, 6$) has a probability $\text{p}_{\text{n}}$. In a large number $\text{N}$ of tosses, the face ``n'' turns up $\text{a}_{\text{n}}$ times, and we say that the probability of getting the face $\text{n}$ is

\begin{equation}
\text{p}_{\text{n}} = \frac{\text{a}_{\text{n}}}{\text{N}}
\label{eq:2B-1}\tag{2B-1}
\end{equation}

Since $\Sigma \text{a}_{\text{n}} = \text{N}$, it follows that

\begin{equation}
\Sigma \text{p}_{\text{n}} = 1
\label{eq:2B-2}\tag{2B-2}
\end{equation}

Let us now assign to each face a certain ``payoff.'' We assign points in the following manner: 1 point when face 1 turns up, 2 points when face 2 turns up, and so on. In $\text{N}$ tosses we get n points $\text{a}_{\text{n}}$ times, so that the total number of points is $\Sigma \text{n} \text{a}_{\text{n}}$. This, of course, grows with $\text{N}$. We thus focus on the average value (points per toss), so that

\begin{equation}
\langle \text{n} \rangle = \frac{1}{\text{N}} \sum_{\text{n}} \text{n} \text{a}_{\text{n}} = \sum_{\text{n}} \text{n} \text{p}_{\text{n}}
\label{eq:2B-3}\tag{2B-3}
\end{equation}

We may be interested in an average of $\text{n}^{2}$, say, and in that case we calculate

\begin{equation}
\langle \text{n}^{2} \rangle = \frac{1}{\text{N}} \sum_{\text{n}} \text{n}^{2} \text{a}_{\text{n}} = \sum_{\text{n}} \text{n}^{2} \text{p}_{\text{n}}
\label{eq:2B-4}\tag{2B-4}
\end{equation}

and so on.

When we do not have a discrete outcome, we must deal with densities. To be specific, consider a quantity that varies continuously, for example the height of a population of students. We can make this discrete by making a histogram (Fig. 2B-1) plotting the height by listing people's heights in 10-cm intervals. Nobody will be exactly 180 cm or 190 cm tall, so we just group people, and somehow round things up at the edges. We may want a finer detail, and list the heights in 1-cm intervals or 1-mm intervals, which will continue to make things discrete, but as the intervals become smaller, the histogram resembles more and more a continuous curve. Let us take some interval, $\text{dx}$, and treat it as infinitesimal. The number of people whose height lies between $\text{x}$ and $\text{x} + \text{dx}$ is $\text{n}(\text{x}) \, \text{dx}$. The proportionality to $\text{dx}$ is obvious: twice as many people will fall into the interval $2\text{dx}$ as fall into $\text{dx}$. It is here that the infinitesimal character of $\text{dx}$ comes in: we do not need to decide whether $\text{n}(\text{x}) \, \text{dx}$ or $\text{n}(\text{x} + \text{dx}/2) \, \text{dx}$ is to be taken, since we treat $(\text{dx})^{2}$ as vanishingly small. If the total population has $\text{N}$ members, we can speak of the probability of falling into the particular interval as

\begin{equation}
\frac{1}{\text{N}} \text{n}(\text{x}) \, \text{dx} = \text{p}(\text{x}) \, \text{dx}
\label{eq:2B-5}\tag{2B-5}
\end{equation}

\hfill W-11",
