&= \frac{1}{a} \int_0^a dx \left\{ \cos\ \frac{(n-m)\pi x}{a} - \ \cos\ \frac{(n+m)\pi x}{a} \right\} \\
&= \frac{\sin\ (n-m)\pi}{(n-m)\pi} - \frac{\sin\ (n+m)\pi}{(n+m)\pi} \qquad \qquad \qquad \text{(3-22)} \\
&= 0 \qquad \text{when } n \neq m \\
&= 1 \qquad \text{when } n = m
\end{aligned}
\end{equation*}

The conclusion that
\begin{equation}
\int_0^a dx\ u_n^*(x) u_m(x) = \delta_{mn} \tag{3-23}
\end{equation}
implies that eigenfunctions corresponding to different eigenvalues are {\it orthogonal}. If
the eigenfunctions are properly normalized, as they are here, (3-23) is called the {\it ortho-
normality condition}. Since the solutions are real, the complex conjugation in that equa-
tion is not really necessary, but is inserted for consistency with the more general
statement that applies when the eigenfunctions are complex. This will be established in
Chapter 5.

We can extract some physical information from the eigensolutions:
\begin{enumerate}
\item {\bf 1.} The state of lowest energy, the {\it ground state}, is described by $u_1(x)$, and the lowest
energy is
\begin{equation}
E_1 = \frac{\pi^2 \hbar^2}{2ma^2} \tag{3-24}
\end{equation}
Note that classically the lowest energy would be that of a particle at rest in the
hole, and for $p = 0$ and $V(x) = 0$, the sum of the kinetic and potential energies
