The time development is given by

\begin{equation}
i\hbar \frac{\partial \psi(x, t)}{\partial t} = H\psi(x, t)
\end{equation}

If we operate with $P$ on this equation, we get

\begin{equation}
i\hbar \frac{\partial}{\partial t} P\psi(x, t) = PH\psi(x, t)
\end{equation}

Under the special circumstances that

\begin{equation}
PH\psi(x, t) = HP\psi(x, t)
\end{equation}

which holds when $H$ is even under $x \rightarrow -x$---that is, when $V(x)$ is an even function (since $d^2/dx^2$ is even)---we have

\begin{equation}
i\hbar \frac{\partial}{\partial t} [P\psi(x, t)] = H[P\psi(x, t)]
\end{equation}

Hence

\begin{equation}
\psi^{(+)}(x, t) = \frac{1}{2}(1 + P)\psi(x, t)
\end{equation}

and

\begin{equation}
\psi^{(-)}(x, t) = \frac{1}{2}(1 - P)\psi(x, t)
\end{equation}

separately obey the Schrödinger equation, and do not mix, if the initial state is even (or odd). The condition for the time independence of parity only holds if

\begin{equation}
(PH - HP)\psi(x, t) = 0
\end{equation}

for all possible states---that is, if the operators $P$ and $H$ commute

\begin{equation}
[P, H] = 0
\end{equation}

This important condition will be seen to be quite general: \textit{any operator that does not have an explicit time dependence and that commutes with the Hamiltonian $H$ is a constant of the motion.} In particular, if the potential changes with time---that is, we have $V(x, t)$---then the energy itself is not a constant of the motion, just as in classical mechanics. Note that when $V$ depends on $t$, the separation of the equation into an equation for the time dependence and an energy eigenvalue equation is not possible.

