The factor $N(\lambda)$ will be determined by the condition $\langle\psi_n|\psi_n\rangle = 1$. We have the freedom to choose the phase of $|\psi_n\rangle$, and we choose it such that the coefficient of $|\phi_n\rangle$ in the expansion is (aside from the normalization factor) unity. Since we require that $|\psi_n\rangle \rightarrow |\phi_n\rangle$ as $\lambda \rightarrow 0$, we must have

\begin{align}
N(0) &= 1 \nonumber \\
C_{nk}(0) &= 0
\tag{11-4}
\end{align}

More generally, we have

\begin{equation}
C_{nk}(\lambda) = \lambda C_{nk}^{(1)} + \lambda^2 C_{nk}^{(2)} + \cdots
\tag{11-5}
\end{equation}

and

\begin{equation}
E_n = E_n^{(0)} + \lambda E_n^{(1)} + \lambda^2 E_n^{(2)} + \cdots
\tag{11-6}
\end{equation}

The Schrödinger energy eigenvalue equation then reads

\begin{align}
(H_0 &+ \lambda H_1) \Bigg\{ |\phi_n\rangle + \lambda \sum_{k\neq n} C_{nk}^{(1)} |\phi_k\rangle + \lambda^2 \sum_{k\neq n} C_{nk}^{(2)} |\phi_k\rangle + \cdots \Bigg\} \nonumber \\
&= (E_n^{(0)} + \lambda E_n^{(1)} + \lambda^2 E_n^{(2)} + \cdots) \Bigg\{ |\phi_n\rangle + \lambda \sum_{k\neq n} C_{nk}^{(1)} |\phi_k\rangle + \lambda^2 \sum_{k\neq n} C_{nk}^{(2)} |\phi_k\rangle + \cdots \Bigg\}
\tag{11-7}
\end{align}

Note that $N(\lambda)$ does not appear in this equation. This is a consequence of the linearity of the equation. Identifying powers of $\lambda$ yields a series of equations. The first one is

\begin{equation}
H_0 \sum_{k\neq n} C_{nk}^{(1)} |\phi_k\rangle + H_1|\phi_n\rangle = E_n^{(0)} \sum_{k\neq n} C_{nk}^{(1)} |\phi_k\rangle + E_n^{(1)} |\phi_n\rangle
\tag{11-8}
\end{equation}

Using $H_0|\phi_n\rangle = E_n^{(0)}|\phi_n\rangle$, we obtain

\begin{equation*}
E_n^{(1)}|\phi_n\rangle = H_1|\phi_n\rangle + \sum_{k\neq n} (E_k^{(0)} - E_n^{(0)}) C_{nk}^{(1)} |\phi_k\rangle
\end{equation*}

If we now take the scalar product with $\langle\phi_n|$ and use the orthonormality condition $\langle\phi_k|\phi_l\rangle = \delta_{kl}$ we obtain

\begin{equation}
\lambda E_n^{(1)} = \langle\phi_n|\lambda H_1|\phi_n\rangle
\tag{11-9}
\end{equation}

This is a *very important formula*. It states that the first-order energy shift for a given state is just the expectation value of the perturbing potential in that state. If the change in the potential is of a definite sign, then the energy shift will have the same sign.

In $x$-space, we have

\begin{equation*}
