{
  "base_latex_md": "11-2 Degenerate Perturbation Theory 179

Let us discuss degenerate perturbation theory further. The problem is always that instead of a unique $|\phi_n\rangle$ there is now a finite set of $|\phi_n^{(i)}\rangle$, all of which have the same energy $E_n^{(0)}$. This set can be made orthonormal with respect to the label “$i$,” because, as we saw in Section 5-4, this label can be associated with the eigenvalues of some other, simultaneously commuting hermitian operator. We thus choose the set of $|\phi_n^{(i)}\rangle$ such that

\begin{equation}
\langle \phi_m^{(i)} | \phi_n^{(j)} \rangle = \delta_{mn} \, \delta_{ij}
\tag{11-21}
\end{equation}

The natural way to take the degeneracy into account is to replace (11-3) by an expression that involves linear combinations of the degenerate eigenfunctions of $H_0$:

\begin{equation}
|\psi_n\rangle = N(\lambda) \left\{ \sum_i \alpha_i |\phi_n^{(i)}\rangle + \lambda \sum_{k \ne n} C_{nk}^{(1)} \sum_i \beta_i |\phi_k^{(i)}\rangle + \dots \right\}
\tag{11-22}
\end{equation}

The coefficients $\alpha_1, \beta_1, \dots$ will have to be determined. When (11-22) is inserted into the Schrödinger equation (11-2), we get, to first order in $\lambda$,

\begin{equation}
H_0 \sum_{k \ne n} C_{nk}^{(1)} \sum_i \beta_i |\phi_k^{(i)}\rangle + H_1 \sum_i \alpha_i |\phi_n^{(i)}\rangle = E_n^{(1)} \sum_i \alpha_i |\phi_n^{(i)}\rangle + E_n^{(0)} \sum_{k \ne n} C_{nk}^{(1)} \sum_i \beta_i |\phi_k^{(i)}\rangle
\tag{11-23}
\end{equation}

Taking the scalar product with $\langle \phi_n^{(j)}|$ gives the first-order shift equation,

\begin{equation}
\sum_i \langle \phi_n^{(j)} | \lambda H_1 | \phi_n^{(i)} \rangle = \lambda E_n^{(1)} \alpha_j
\tag{11-24}
\end{equation}

This is a finite-dimensional eigenvalue problem. For example, if there is a twofold degeneracy, and if we use the notation $\langle \phi_n^{(j)} | H_1 | \phi_n^{(i)} \rangle = h_{ji}$, the equation reads

\begin{align*}
h_{11}\alpha_1 + h_{12}\alpha_2 &= E_n^{(1)} \alpha_1 \\
h_{21}\alpha_1 + h_{22}\alpha_2 &= E_n^{(1)} \alpha_2
\end{align*}

The eigenvalues and the $\alpha_i$ can be determined from this equation, if we add the condition that

\begin{equation*}
\sum_i |\alpha_i|^2 = 1
\end{equation*}

We do not bother with the determination of the $\beta_i$, since we shall only use degenerate perturbation theory for the first-order energy eigenvalues in our applications. If it so happens that $h_{ij} = 0$ for $i \ne j$—that is, the matrix $h_{ij}$ is diagonal—then the first-order shifts are just the diagonal elements of this matrix. This will happen when the perturbation $H_1$ commutes with the operator whose eigenvalues the “$i$” labels represent. For example, in the hydrogen atom, there is a degeneracy associated with the eigenvalues of $L_z$; that is, all $m$-values have the same energy. If it happens that

\begin{equation}
[H_1, L_z] = 0
\tag{11-25}
\end{equation}

and if we choose our $\phi_n^{(i)}$ to be eigenfunctions of $L_z$, then $h_{ij}$ will be diagonal. To see this, note that with

\begin{align}
L_z |\phi_n^{(i)}\rangle &= \hbar m^{(i)} |\phi_n^{(i)}\rangle \notag\\
\langle \phi_n^{(j)} | [H_1, L_z] | \phi_n^{(i)} \rangle &= \langle \phi_n^{(j)} | H_1 L_z - L_z H_1 | \phi_n^{(i)} \rangle \notag\\
&= \hbar (m^{(i)} - m^{(j)}) h_{ji} \notag\\
&= 0
\tag{11-26}
\end{align}",
