\section*{The Real Hydrogen Atom}

\textbf{T}he discussion of hydrogenlike atoms in Chapter 8 was based on the Hamitonian
\begin{equation}
H_0 = \frac{\mathbf{p}^2}{2\mu} - \frac{Ze^2}{4\pi\varepsilon_0}\frac{1}{r}
\tag{12-1}
\end{equation}
In a more realistic treatment, several corrections must be taken into account. We separate
out the treatment of the proton motion so as not to clutter up our discussion of relativistic
and spin effects with simple kinematic matters that involve the motion of the proton. For
the time being we treat the proton as infinitely massive, and return to the effect of its
motion in a separate section.

\section*{12-1 RELATIVISTIC KINETIC ENERGY EFFECTS}

The relativistic expression for the kinetic energy of the electron is
\begin{equation}
K = \sqrt{(\mathbf{pc})^2 + (m_ec^2)^2} - m_ec^2 \approx \frac{\mathbf{p}^2}{2m_e} - \frac{1}{8}\frac{(\mathbf{p}^2)^2}{m_e^3c^2} + \cdots
\tag{12-2}
\end{equation}
The second term is the perturbation we want to consider. It gives rise to
\begin{equation}
H_1 = -\frac{1}{2}\left(\frac{\mathbf{p}^2}{2m_e}\right)^2\frac{1}{m_ec^2}
\tag{12-3}
\end{equation}
We can estimate its effect on the basic energy levels. We know that $\mathbf{p}^2/2m_e \approx O(\alpha^2m_ec^2)$
so that the perturbation is of order $\alpha^2$ smaller than the leading term. We will evaluate it
more accurately a little later.

\section*{12-2 SPIN-ORBIT COUPLING}

The spin of the electron and its associated magnetic moment change the interaction of the
electron with the electric field due to the proton charge. An electron moving with uniform
velocity $\mathbf{v}$ experiences a magnetic field
\begin{equation}
\mathbf{B} = -\frac{\mathbf{v} \times \mathbf{E}}{c^2} = -\frac{\mathbf{v} \times (-\nabla\phi(r))}{c^2} = \frac{1}{c^2}\mathbf{v} \times \frac{\mathbf{r}}{r}\frac{d\phi(r)}{dr}
\tag{12-4}
\end{equation}
where $\phi(r)$ is the potential due to the proton charge. The magnetic moment of the electron
interacts with this magnetic field. With
\begin{equation*}
\boldsymbol{\mu} = -\frac{e}{2m_e}g\mathbf{S} = -\frac{e}{m_e}\mathbf{S}
\end{equation*}

