\vspace{1cm}

{\huge Conservation of Total Momentum}

\vspace{1cm}

In our discussion of angular momentum in Chapter 8 we found that the assumption of invariance of the Hamiltonian under rotations led to the appearance of a new constant of motion, the angular momentum. In this supplement we show that the assumption of invariance under spatial displacement leads to the existence of a constant of the motion, the momentum. The requirement that the system be unchanged under the transformation

\begin{equation}
x_i \rightarrow x_i + a
\tag{13A-1}
\end{equation}

does not change the kinetic energy, since a is independent of time. The potential energy will change, unless it has the form

\begin{equation}
V(x_1, x_2, x_3, \dots, x_N) = V(x_1 - x_2, x_1 - x_3 \dots x_2 - x_3, \dots, x_{N-1} - x_N)
\tag{13A-2}
\end{equation}

In classical mechanics, the absence of external forces leads to momentum conservation. This follows from the equations of motion,

\begin{equation}
m_i \frac{d^2x_i}{dt^2} = -\frac{\partial}{\partial x_i} V(x_1 - x_2, x_1 - x_3, \dots, x_{N-1} - x_N)
\tag{13A-3}
\end{equation}

a consequence of which is that

\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
\frac{d}{dt} \sum_i m_i \frac{dx_i}{dt} & = - \sum_i \frac{\partial}{\partial x_i} V(x_1 - x_2, \dots, x_{N-1} - x_N) \\
& = 0
\end{aligned}
\tag{13A-4}
\end{equation}

The reason for the vanishing of the right side of the preceding equation is that for every argument in $V$, there are equal and opposite contributions that come from $\sum_i \partial/\partial x_i$ acting on it. For example, with $u = x_1 - x_2, v = x_1 - x_3, w = x_2 - x_3$,

\begin{equation*}
\left( \frac{\partial}{\partial x_1} + \frac{\partial}{\partial x_2} + \frac{\partial}{\partial x_3} \right) V(u, v, w) = \frac{\partial V}{\partial u} + \frac{\partial V}{\partial v} - \frac{\partial V}{\partial u} + \frac{\partial V}{\partial w} - \frac{\partial V}{\partial v} - \frac{\partial V}{\partial w} = 0
\end{equation*}

Hence

\begin{equation}
P = \sum_i m_i \frac{dx_i}{dt}
\tag{13A-5}
\end{equation}

is a constant of the motion.

In quantum mechanics the same conclusion holds. We shall demonstrate it by using the invariance of the Hamiltonian under the transformation (13A-1). The invariance implies that both

\begin{equation}
\text{H} u_{\text{E}}(x_1, x_2, \dots, x_{\text{N}}) = \text{E} u_{\text{E}}(x_1, x_2, \dots, x_{\text{N}})
\tag{13A-6}
\end{equation}

and

\begin{equation}
\text{H} u_{\text{E}}(x_1 + \text{a}, x_2 + \text{a}, \dots, x_{\text{N}} + \text{a}) = \text{E} u_{\text{E}}(x_1 + \text{a}, x_2 + \text{a}, \dots, x_{\text{N}} + \text{a})
\tag{13A-7}
\end{equation}

