\subsection*{Molecular Orbitals}

A reasonable trial wave function is a linear combination of $u_{100}(r_1, \mathbf{R})$ and $u_{100}(r_2, \mathbf{R})$, the ground-state wave functions of electrons bound to one or the other of the protons. Here $r_1 = |\mathbf{r} - \mathbf{R}/2|$ and $r_2 = |\mathbf{r} + \mathbf{R}/2|$. Since the two nuclei are identical, the Hamiltonian is symmetric under reflections in the plane bisecting the line joining them. This means that the Hamiltonian is invariant under reflections $\mathbf{r} \to -\mathbf{r}$. We may therefore choose linear combinations that are also eigenfunctions of parity, and therefore choose as our trial wave functions the so-called \textit{molecular orbitals}

\begin{align}
\psi_e(\mathbf{r}, \mathbf{R}) &= C_+(\psi_1 + \psi_2) \notag \\
\psi_o(\mathbf{r}, \mathbf{R}) &= C_-(\psi_1 - \psi_2) \tag{14-51}
\end{align}

where

\begin{equation}
\psi_{1,2} = \left(\frac{1}{\pi a_0^3}\right)^{1/2} e^{-|\mathbf{r} \mp \mathbf{R}/2|/a_0} \tag{14-52}
\end{equation}

The normalization factors $C_{\pm}$ are given by

\begin{equation}
\frac{1}{C_{\pm}} = \langle \psi_1 \pm \psi_2 | \psi_1 \pm \psi_2 \rangle = 2 \pm 2 \int d^3r \psi_1(\mathbf{r}, \mathbf{R}) \psi_2(\mathbf{r}, \mathbf{R}) \tag{14-53}
\end{equation}

The integral appearing above is called the \textit{overlap integral}. The calculation of

\begin{align}
S(R) &= \int d^3r \psi_1(\mathbf{r}, \mathbf{R}) \psi_2(\mathbf{r}, \mathbf{R}) \notag \\
&= \frac{1}{\pi a_0^3} \int d^3r e^{-|\mathbf{r} - \mathbf{R}/2|/a_0} e^{-|\mathbf{r} + \mathbf{R}/2|/a_0} \tag{14-54} \\
&= \frac{1}{\pi a_0^3} \int d^3r' e^{-|\mathbf{r}' - \mathbf{R}|/a_0} e^{-r'/a_0} \notag
\end{align}",
